'''Gliese 436 b''' {{IPAc-en|ˈ|ɡ|l|iː|z|ə}} (sometimes called '''GJ 436 b''',<ref name="arXiv-20120801"/> formally named '''Awohali'''<ref name="NEW2022"/>) is a [[Neptune]]-sized [[exoplanet]] orbiting the [[red dwarf]] [[Gliese 436]] (Noquisi), the only known planet in its system.<ref name="Lanotte2014"/> It was the first [[hot Neptune]] discovered with certainty (in 2007) and was among the smallest-known [[transiting planet]]s in mass and radius, until the much smaller [[Kepler (spacecraft)|Kepler]] exoplanet discoveries began [[Wiktionary:circa|circa]] 2010.

In December 2013, NASA reported that [[clouds]] may have been detected in the [[Exoplanet#Atmosphere|atmosphere]] of GJ 436 b.<ref name="NASA-20131231">{{cite web |last1=Harrington |first1=J.D. |last2=Weaver |first2=Donna |last3=Villard |first3=Ray |title=Release 13-383 - NASA's Hubble Sees Cloudy Super-Worlds With Chance for More Clouds |url=http://www.nasa.gov/press/2013/december/nasas-hubble-sees-cloudy-super-worlds-with-chance-for-more-clouds/ |date=December 31, 2013 |work=[[NASA]] |access-date=January 1, 2014 |archive-date=January 2, 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140102191652/http://www.nasa.gov/press/2013/december/nasas-hubble-sees-cloudy-super-worlds-with-chance-for-more-clouds/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name="NAT-20140101a">{{cite journal |last=Moses |first=Julianne |title=Extrasolar planets: Cloudy with a chance of dustballs |date=January 1, 2014 |journal=[[Nature (journal)|Nature]] |volume=505 |pages=31–32 |doi=10.1038/505031a |bibcode = 2014Natur.505...31M |issue=7481 |pmid=24380949|s2cid=4408861 }}</ref><ref name="NAT-20140101b">{{cite journal |author=Knutson, Heather |display-authors=etal |title=A featureless transmission spectrum for the Neptune-mass exoplanet GJ 436b |date=January 1, 2014 |journal=[[Nature (journal)|Nature]] |volume=505 |pages=66–68 |doi=10.1038/nature12887 |arxiv = 1401.3350 |bibcode = 2014Natur.505...66K |issue=7481 |pmid=24380953|s2cid=4454617 }}</ref><ref name="NAT-20140101c">{{cite journal |author=Kreidberg, Laura |display-authors=etal |title=Clouds in the atmosphere of the super-Earth exoplanet GJ 1214b |date=January 1, 2014 |journal=[[Nature (journal)|Nature]] |volume=505 |pages=69–72 |doi=10.1038/nature12888 |arxiv = 1401.0022 |bibcode = 2014Natur.505...69K |issue=7481 |pmid=24380954|hdl=1721.1/118780 |s2cid=4447642 }}</ref>

==Discovery==
Gliese 436 b was discovered in August 2004 by [[R.&nbsp;Paul Butler]] and [[Geoffrey Marcy]] of the [[Carnegie Institution for Science|Carnegie Institute of Washington]] and [[University of California, Berkeley]], respectively, using the [[methods of detecting extrasolar planets|radial velocity method]]. Together with [[55 Cancri&nbsp;e]], it was the first of a new class of planets with a minimum mass (M&nbsp;sin''i'') similar to Neptune.<ref name="Butler2004"/>

The planet was recorded to [[astronomical transit|transit]] its star by an automatic process at [[NMSU]] on January&nbsp;11, 2005, but this event went unheeded at the time.<ref name=coughlin>{{cite journal|title=New observations and a possible detection of parameter variations in the transits of Gliese 436b| date=2008|last1=Coughlin | first1=Jeffrey L.|last2=Stringfellow | first2=Guy S.|last3=Becker | first3=Andrew C.|author4=Mercedes Lopez-Morales|author5=Fabio Mezzalira|author6=Tom Krajci|doi=10.1086/595822|journal=The Astrophysical Journal|volume=689|issue=2|pages=L149–L152| arxiv=0809.1664 | bibcode=2008ApJ...689L.149C| s2cid=14893633}}</ref> In 2007, Michael Gillon from [[University of Geneva|Geneva University]] in [[Switzerland]] led a team that observed the transit, grazing the stellar disc relative to Earth. Transit observations led to the determination of its exact mass and radius, both of which are very similar to that of Neptune, making Gliese 436 b at that time the smallest known transiting extrasolar planet. The planet is about four thousand kilometers larger in diameter than Uranus and five thousand kilometers larger than Neptune and slightly more massive. Gliese 436 b orbits at a distance of four million kilometers or one-fifteenth the average distance of [[Mercury (planet)|Mercury]] from the [[Sun]].<ref name=gillon/>

==Naming==
In August 2022, this planet and its host star were included among 20 systems to be named by the third [[NameExoWorlds]] project.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.nameexoworlds.iau.org/2022exoworlds |title=List of ExoWorlds 2022 |date=8 August 2022 |website=nameexoworlds.iau.org |publisher=[[International Astronomical Union|IAU]] |access-date=27 August 2022 |archive-date=8 March 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230308103542/https://www.nameexoworlds.iau.org/2022exoworlds |url-status=live }}</ref> The approved names, proposed by a team from the [[United States]], were announced in June 2023. Gliese 436 b is named Awohali and its host star is named Noquisi, after the [[Cherokee language|Cherokee]] words for "eagle" and "star". As stated in the IAU naming citation, the name refers to a Cherokee legend, alluding to the planet's evaporating atmosphere:<ref name="NEW2022"/>

{{blockquote|“Awohali” ({{lang|chr|ᎠᏬᎭᎵ}}, Ah-Wo-Ha-Lee) is one of the Cherokee words for “eagle”. According to Cherokee legend, an eagle flew to the Sun to deliver a prayer for a warrior. In delivering the prayer to the Sun, the Sun kissed the tail feather of the eagle and had him return the feather to the warrior as a symbol of the connection between his people and the Great Spirit. Awohali’s sun-kissed tail feather alludes to the comet-like cloud of evaporating atmosphere detected around the exoplanet GJ 436 b.}}

==Physical characteristics==
[[File:GJ436interior.jpg|thumb|left|Possible interior structure of Gliese 436 b]]
[[File:PIA19345-HeliumAtmosphereFormation-20150611.jpg|thumb|left|{{center|Formation of a [[helium]] [[atmosphere]] on a [[helium planet]], possibly like Gliese 436 b.}}]]
The planet's surface temperature is estimated from measurements taken as it passes behind the star to be {{convert|712|K}}.<ref name="deming">{{cite journal|title=Spitzer Transit and Secondary Eclipse Photometry of GJ 436b|date=2007|author1=Drake Deming|author2=Joseph Harrington|author3=Gregory Laughlin|author4=Sara Seager|last5=Navarro | first5=Sarah B.|last6=Bowman | first6=William C.|author7=Karen Horning|doi=10.1086/522496|journal=The Astrophysical Journal|volume=667|issue=2|pages=L199–L202|arxiv=0707.2778|bibcode=2007ApJ...667L.199D|s2cid=13349666}}</ref> This temperature is significantly higher than would be expected if the planet were only heated by radiation from its star, which was prior to this measurement, estimated at 520&nbsp;K. Whatever energy tidal effects deliver to the planet, it does not affect its temperature significantly.<ref>{{cite journal| title=Tidal Heating of Extra-Solar Planets|date=2008| author1=Brian Jackson| author2=Richard Greenberg| author3=Rory Barnes| doi=10.1086/587641| journal=The Astrophysical Journal| volume=681| issue=2| pages=1631–1638 |arxiv=0803.0026| bibcode=2008ApJ...681.1631J|s2cid=42315630}}</ref> A [[greenhouse effect]] would result in a much greater temperature than the predicted 520–620 K.<ref name=gillon/>

Its main constituent was initially predicted to be hot "[[ice]]" in various exotic high-pressure forms,<ref name=gillon /><ref name="hot ice">{{cite news|publisher=New Scientist |url=https://www.newscientist.com/article/dn11864-strange-alien-world-made-of-hot-ice-and-steam.html |title=Strange alien world made of "hot ice" |date=6 May 2007 |first=David |last=Shiga |access-date=2007-05-16 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080706143705/http://space.newscientist.com/article/dn11864-strange-alien-world-made-of-hot-ice-and-steam.html |archive-date=July 6, 2008 }}</ref> which would remain solid despite the high temperatures, because of the planet's gravity.<ref>{{cite news | title = Hot "ice" may cover recently discovered planet | first = Maggie | last = Fox | publisher = Scientific American.com | work = Science News | date = May 16, 2007 | url = https://www.reuters.com/article/scienceNews/idUSN1621607620070516 | access-date = 2008-08-06 | archive-date = 2012-09-07 | archive-url = https://archive.today/20120907134721/http://www.reuters.com/article/scienceNews/idUSN1621607620070516 | url-status = live }}</ref> The planet could have formed further from its current position, as a gas giant, and migrated inwards with the other gas giants. As it approached its present position, radiation from the star would have blown off the planet's hydrogen layer via [[coronal mass ejection]].<ref>{{cite journal | journal=Geophysical Research Abstracts | volume=9 | issue=7850 | date=2007 | url=http://www.cosis.net/abstracts/EGU2007/07850/EGU2007-J-07850.pdf?PHPSESSID=1eb3a7a98603083dda25d18001ea2a33 | title=The impact of nonthermal loss processes on planet masses from Neptunes to Jupiters | author=H. Lammer | display-authors=etal | access-date=2008-08-18 | archive-date=2019-12-15 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191215090631/https://www.cosis.net/abstracts/EGU2007/07850/EGU2007-J-07850.pdf?PHPSESSID=1eb3a7a98603083dda25d18001ea2a33 | url-status=live }} By analogy with [[Gliese 876 d]].</ref>

However, when the radius became better known, ice alone was not enough to account for the observed size. An outer layer of [[hydrogen]] and [[helium]], accounting for up to ten percent of the mass, was needed on top of the ice to account for the observed planetary radius.<ref name="deming" /><ref name="bean">{{Cite journal|url=http://www.aanda.org/index.php?option=article&access=standard&Itemid=129&url=/articles/aa/abs/2008/30/aa10013-08/aa10013-08.html|author=Bean, J.L.|display-authors=etal|journal=Astronomy & Astrophysics|title=A Hubble Space Telescope transit light curve for GJ 436b|date=2008|doi=10.1051/0004-6361:200810013|doi-access=free|bibcode=2008A&A...486.1039B|volume=486|issue=3|pages=1039–1046|arxiv=0806.0851|s2cid=6351375|access-date=2008-08-07|archive-date=2010-04-08|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100408055429/http://www.aanda.org/index.php?option=article&access=standard&Itemid=129&url=%2Farticles%2Faa%2Fabs%2F2008%2F30%2Faa10013-08%2Faa10013-08.html|url-status=live}}</ref> This obviates the need for an ice core. Alternatively, the planet may consist of a dense rocky core surrounded by a lesser amount of hydrogen.<ref>{{cite journal| title=Ocean Planet or Thick Atmosphere: On the Mass-Radius Relationship for Solid Exoplanets with Massive Atmospheres |author1=E. R. Adams |author2=S. Seager |author3=L. Elkins-Tanton | journal=[[The Astrophysical Journal]] | volume=673 | issue=2 | pages=1160–1164 |date=February 2008 | doi=10.1086/524925 | bibcode=2008ApJ...673.1160A|arxiv = 0710.4941 |s2cid=6676647 }}</ref>

Observations of the planet's [[brightness temperature]] with the [[Spitzer Space Telescope]] suggest a possible thermochemical disequilibrium in the atmosphere of this exoplanet. Results published in Nature suggest that Awohali’s dayside atmosphere is abundant in CO and deficient in methane (CH<sub>4</sub>) by a factor of ~7,000.  This result is unexpected because, based on current models at its temperature, atmospheric carbon should prefer CH<sub>4</sub> over CO.<ref>{{cite journal | title= Possible thermochemical disequilibrium in the atmosphere of the exoplanet GJ 436b |journal= Nature|volume= 464|pages= 1161–1164| date=22 April 2010|arxiv = 1010.4591 |bibcode = 2010Natur.464.1161S |doi = 10.1038/nature09013 | pmid=20414304 | issue=7292 | last1 = Stevenson | first1 = KB | last2 = Harrington | first2 = J | last3 = Nymeyer | first3 = S |s2cid= 4416249|display-authors=etal}}</ref><ref>[http://planets.ucf.edu/research/exoplanets/GJ436b-Wheres-the-methane GJ436b - Where's the methane?] {{webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100514162550/http://planets.ucf.edu/research/exoplanets/GJ436b-Wheres-the-methane |date=2010-05-14 }} Planetary Sciences Group at the University of Central Florida, Orlando</ref><ref name=Knutson>{{cite journal| title=A ''Spitzer'' Transmission Spectrum for the Exoplanet GJ 436b |date=2011| last1=Knutson | first1=Heather A.| doi=10.1088/0004-637X/735/1/27| journal=Astrophysical Journal| volume=735, 27 |issue=1|arxiv=1104.2901 |bibcode = 2011ApJ...735...27K | pages=27|s2cid=18669291}}</ref><ref name=Line>{{cite journal| title=Thermochemical and Photochemical Kinetics in Cooler Hydrogen Dominated Extrasolar Planets |date=2011| last1=LINE | first1=Michael R. | last2=VASISHT | first2=Gautam| last3=CHEN | first3=Pin| last4=ANGERHAUSEN | first4=D.| last5=YANG  | first5=Yuk L.| journal=Astrophysical Journal| arxiv=1104.3183| doi=10.1088/0004-637X/738/1/32|volume=738, 32|issue=1|bibcode = 2011ApJ...738...32L | pages=32|s2cid=15087062}}, abstract in the arXiv titled "Thermochemistry and Photochemistry in Cooler Hydrogen Dominated Extrasolar Planets: The Case of GJ436b"</ref> In part for this reason, it has also been hypothesized to be a possible [[helium planet]].<ref name=SpaceDaily-2015-06-16>{{cite news |url= http://www.spacedaily.com/reports/Helium_Shrouded_Planets_May_Be_Common_in_Our_Galaxy_999.html |title= Helium-Shrouded Planets May Be Common in Our Galaxy |publisher= SpaceDaily |date= 16 June 2015 |access-date= 3 August 2015 |archive-date= 11 August 2019 |archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20190811113518/http://www.spacedaily.com/reports/Helium_Shrouded_Planets_May_Be_Common_in_Our_Galaxy_999.html |url-status= live }}</ref>

In June 2015, scientists reported that the atmosphere of Awohali was evaporating,<ref>{{cite journal| title=A Giant Comet-like Cloud of Hydrogen Escaping from the warm Neptune-mass Exoplanet GJ 436b |author1=D. Ehrenreich |author2=V. Bourrier |author3=P. Wheatley |author4=A. Lecavelier des Etangs |author5=G. Hébrard |author6=S. Udry |author7=X. Bonfils |author8=X. Delfosse |author9=J.-M. Désert |author10=D. K. Sing |author11=A. Vidal-Madjar | journal=[[Nature (journal)|Nature]] | volume=522 | issue=7557 | pages=459–461 |date=25 June 2015 | doi=10.1038/nature14501 | bibcode=2015Natur.522..459E|arxiv = 1506.07541 | pmid=26108854|s2cid=4388969 }}</ref> resulting in a giant cloud around the planet and, due to radiation from the host star, a long trailing tail {{convert|9|e6mi|e6km|order=flip|abbr=on}} long.<ref name="NYT-20150625">{{cite news |last=Bhanoo |first=Sindya N. |title=A Planet with a Tail Nine Million Miles Long |url=https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/projects/cp/summer-of-science-2015/latest/exoplanet-tail |date=25 June 2015 |work=[[New York Times]] |access-date=25 June 2015 |archive-date=25 June 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150625153820/http://www.nytimes.com/interactive/projects/cp/summer-of-science-2015/latest/exoplanet-tail |url-status=live }}</ref>
[[File:Gliese 436 b.tiff|thumb|right|250px|Artist impression of Gliese 436b shows the enormous comet-like cloud of hydrogen boiling off.<ref>{{cite web|title=Hubble sees atmosphere being stripped from Neptune-sized exoplanet|url=http://www.spacetelescope.org/news/heic1515/|access-date=25 June 2015}}</ref>]]

==Orbital characteristics==
One orbit around the star takes only about two [[day]]s, 15.5 [[hour]]s. Awohali orbit is likely misaligned with its star's rotation.<ref name="Knutson" /> The eccentricity of Awohali’s orbit is inconsistent with models of planetary system evolution. To have maintained its eccentricity over time requires that it be accompanied by another planet.<ref name="deming" /><ref>{{Cite journal|title=Observational Consequences of the Recently Proposed Super-Earth Orbiting GJ436|journal=Astronomy & Astrophysics|volume=487|issue=2|pages=L25–L28|arxiv=0806.3270|date=2008|last1=Bean | first1=Jacob L.|author2=Andreas Seifahrt|doi=10.1051/0004-6361:200810278|doi-access=free|bibcode=2008A&A...487L..25B|s2cid=14811323}}</ref>

A study published in ''[[Nature (journal)|Nature]]'' found that the orbit of Awohali is nearly perpendicular (inclined by  103.2{{±|12.8|11.5}} degrees)<ref>{{citation|arxiv=2203.06109|year=2022|title=The polar orbit of the warm Neptune GJ 436b seen with VLT/ESPRESSO|doi=10.1051/0004-6361/202142559 |last1=Bourrier |first1=V. |last2=Zapatero Osorio |first2=M. R. |last3=Allart |first3=R. |last4=Attia |first4=O. |last5=Cretignier |first5=M. |last6=Dumusque |first6=X. |last7=Lovis |first7=C. |last8=Adibekyan |first8=V. |last9=Borsa |first9=F. |last10=Figueira |first10=P. |last11=Hernández |first11=J. I. González |last12=Mehner |first12=A. |last13=Santos |first13=N. C. |last14=Schmidt |first14=T. |last15=Seidel |first15=J. V. |last16=Sozzetti |first16=A. |last17=Alibert |first17=Y. |last18=Casasayas-Barris |first18=N. |last19=Ehrenreich |first19=D. |last20=Lo Curto |first20=G. |last21=Martins |first21=C. J. A. P. |last22=Di Marcantonio |first22=P. |last23=Mégevand |first23=D. |last24=Nunes |first24=N. J. |last25=Palle |first25=E. |last26=Poretti |first26=E. |last27=Sousa |first27=S. G. |journal=Astronomy & Astrophysics |volume=663 |pages=A160 |doi-access=free |bibcode=2022A&A...663A.160B |s2cid=247139822 }}</ref> to the stellar equator of Noquisi and suggests that the eccentricity and misalignment of the orbit could have resulted from interactions with a yet undetected companion.  The inward migration caused by this interaction could have triggered the atmospheric escape that sustains its giant exosphere.<ref name="Bourrier 2017"/>

